h1 -- kbd privacy policy

p -- Effective October 14, 2026

p --
  Nothing you type leaves your device. The keyboard has no network access, and
  kbd has no accounts, no analytics and no tracking, and does not use Full
  Access. kbd collects no data, unless you choose to send swipes from Train
  swipes, below.

p --
  To work, kbd stores a few small files on your iPhone, inside the keyboard's
  own storage: words you add, email addresses you choose to remember, your emoji
  history, and numbers describing where your thumb lands on the keys. They never
  leave your phone, and deleting the app deletes them. kbd also reads your Text
  Replacements and contact names from iOS to offer as suggestions, and keeps
  them only in memory.

h2 -- Train swipes

p --
  Train swipes, in the kbd app, asks you to swipe sentences it shows you, to
  help improve swipe typing. It is for people aged 18 or over, and is off until
  you agree. Nothing leaves your phone until you tap Send. Then the app sends
  this site where your finger moved and when, for each swipe, and how many tries
  each word took; the sentences you were asked to swipe; the finger and the
  setting you chose; and your phone's model, its screen size and the app's
  version. It sends them under a random ID the app made, which names neither you
  nor your phone, and sends nothing else. Your IP address is used only to limit
  how often requests are accepted, and is not stored. After 30 days, what you
  sent may be published as an open dataset.

p --
  To delete what you sent, enter the ID shown on Train swipes at
  lavalva.dev/app/kbd/training. That can't recall a copy already published.
  Nothing else names your swipes, so keep the ID if you may want them deleted
  after you delete the app.

p -- Questions: kbd@lavalva.dev
